## 4. Compilation & Deployment

### Step 4.1: Compile the Custom System Binary

Configure and build only the required `x86_64` virtualization targets to speed up compilation time:

```bash
mkdir build
cd build
../configure --target-list=x86_64-softmmu --enable-kvm --enable-gtk
make -j$(nproc)
```

### Step 4.2: Move and Register the Binary on the Target Host

1. Transfer the compiled `qemu-system-x86_64` binary to the host machine via secure network copy or standard USB media storage.
2. Store the binary in a distinct local directory to prevent accidental package manager overwrites:

```bash
sudo cp qemu-system-x86_64 /usr/local/bin/mife-qemu-system-x86_64
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/mife-qemu-system-x86_64
```

3. Grant raw I/O privileges to the binary. Because standard users cannot execute raw hardware assembly inb/outb loops, grant the new binary raw I/O capabilities using Linux file traits:

```Bash
sudo setcap cap_sys_rawio+ep /usr/local/bin/mife-qemu-system-x86_64
```

### Step 4.3: Updating Your Domain XML Configuration within Libvirt (Virtual Machine Manager)

In QEMU, property assignments are added directly to the device call string separated by commas. Boolean options can be set using on/off or true/false. QEMU accepts both base-10 integers and traditional hexadecimal prefix formats (0x) for the ports.

Open your virtual machine XML configuration profile and edit the bottom block, add following lines just before the </domain>. You can adjust the debug/host-port/guest-port as appropriate:

```Bash
  <qemu:commandline>
    <qemu:arg value='-device'/>
    <qemu:arg value='mife-host-isa-proxy,debug=off,host-port=0x5390,guest-port=0x390'/>
    
    <qemu:arg value='-rtc'/>
    <qemu:arg value='base=localtime,clock=host,driftfix=slew'/>
  </qemu:commandline>
</domain>
```

The second part of the above lines (the '-rtc' parts) is the most stable, battle-tested configuration for running Windows 98 with hardware virtualization (KVM) on a Linux host uses the host's local wall-clock time, paired with a mechanism to gently handle missed ticks.
driftfix=slew: This is the magic flag. If the VM temporarily falls behind because of a heavy burst of data from your ISA card, QEMU will smoothly inject the missing clock ticks at a slightly accelerated rate to catch up, rather than dumping them all into Windows 98 at once and locking up the OS.

